# What CI downloads, checks and installs before and during the gate (ADR-0017): CI runs the pull request's own copy of these files, so a change here decides what CI fetches and runs, and how it is checked. Every file in these two folders is such a file, and a new one is covered as soon as it is added.
# deps/ holds the pins. ubuntu-packages.lock names the base image of the Linux CI jobs and lists every Ubuntu package file that they install, with its size and SHA-256, taken from the package index that apt checked against Ubuntu's signature: those hashes are the only link between the files and that signature. qt.json names the Qt release, its date and the archives to install, which carry no hashes in this repository, so the installer checks them against those that download.qt.io publishes. bayan-core.json names the repository and the commit whose C SDK CI builds, and with them the Rust version and the crates that the commit pins; the repository must stay https://github.com/BayanDocs/bayan-core.git, because build-engine-sdk.sh accepts any GitHub repository and looks for the commit on that repository's own main, so a changed URL makes CI fetch and build another repository's code. requirements-tools.txt pins CMake, Ninja, clang-format and clang-tidy with their SHA-256.
/deps/ @Gabrielc1925
# scripts/ holds the programs that act on the pins. ubuntu-packages.sh fetches the Ubuntu package files, checks them against the lock and installs them with dpkg, which runs the packages' maintainer scripts as root in the CI containers. check-qt-release-age.py stops every job that installs Qt, before it does, unless the pinned release is at least 24 hours old; install-qt.py downloads Qt's archives, checks each against its published SHA-256 and every entry for unsafe paths and links, and extracts them; dev-setup.sh installs the pinned tools with pip --require-hashes and Qt with install-qt.py. build-engine-sdk.sh fetches the pinned commit from the pinned repository, checks that it is on that repository's main, installs that commit's Rust version with rustup and builds the C SDK; test-engine-sdk.sh builds the app against that SDK, or against a stand-in built from the stub engine, and runs the tests that use the engine.
/scripts/ @Gabrielc1925
# The verification gate's definition, code and configuration (cmake --workflow --preset verify, and CI's other presets): CI runs a pull request's own copy of them, so a change here can weaken the checks that judge it. CMakePresets.json defines the gate and CI's builds: warnings as errors, the lint, the sanitizers' settings and which tests run against the real engine. Every CMakeLists.txt and *.cmake file runs inside the gate's configuration, where CMake lets each one change what is configured after it, for example cancel the deferred Qt licensing check or leave tests unregistered, so all of them are listed: the root CMakeLists.txt includes the Qt licensing policy, the compiler settings and the lint, and chooses which files and test programs the lint covers; each target opts in to bayan::compile_options in its own CMakeLists.txt, the app's in src/CMakeLists.txt, the engine wrapper's in src/engine/CMakeLists.txt and the tests' in tests/CMakeLists.txt, which also registers every test of the gate, among them the supply-chain and licensing checks, and decides which of them may skip instead of fail. In cmake/, which is listed as a folder too, so that a file of any kind added there is covered, BayanQtPolicy.cmake is the Qt licensing policy: only reviewed LGPL modules, linked dynamically, and never the commercial qmlsc (ADR-0013); BayanCompilerOptions.cmake sets, for every target that links bayan::compile_options, the warnings that fail the build, the hardening of the binaries and the sanitizers; BayanLint.cmake runs clang-format and clang-tidy, checks that they are exactly the versions in deps/requirements-tools.txt, and fails on any finding.
/CMakePresets.json @Gabrielc1925
